# Supply chain cooldown. A version published fewer than this many days ago is not installable here,
# because inside that window "no advisory yet" means detection has not opened, not that a package is
# clean. npm audit being quiet about a package published this morning is not evidence.
#
# WHO THIS ACTUALLY COVERS, stated precisely because the first version of this file overclaimed by
# naming CI. The key governs dependency RESOLUTION, so it applies to `npm install` and not to
# `npm ci`, which reads the lockfile and resolves nothing. It also needs npm 11.10.0 or newer:
# `min-release-age` arrived in npm/cli #8965 on 2026-02-11, and an older npm ignores an unknown key
# in SILENCE. Measured on 10.9.8: no warning, no message, exit 0, for this key and for a deliberate
# nonsense one, while 11.14.1 does warn about the nonsense one. So on an old npm there is nothing
# at all to notice. CI runs `npm ci` on the Node 22 line, which bundles npm 10.9.x, so this setting
# is doubly irrelevant there and was never what protects CI. What protects CI is the committed
# lockfile plus `npm run gate:advisories`.
#
# So it covers the case it was written for: a human or an agent adding a dependency on a current
# npm. `engines.npm` in package.json names the version that makes it real, which is the only thing
# turning a too-old npm into something visible rather than silent.
#
# It lives in the REPOSITORY and not only in a developer's ~/.npmrc, which is where it was until a
# review pointed out the gap: docs/security-notes.md described a protection that did not exist for
# anyone else who cloned this, and one of its own dated actions told a reader to run
# `npm install hono@latest` on a machine where nothing would hold that install back.
#
# Measured before committing, because a cooldown that broke every CI run would be a worse problem
# than the one it solves: with this set to 30, `npm ci` on a tree with no node_modules still
# installed the locked versions and exited 0.
#
# It is never bypassed to clear an advisory faster. When a fix is published inside the window, the
# answer is an entry in scripts/accepted-advisories.json with a short recheck date.
#
# NOTHING SECRET GOES IN THIS FILE. It is tracked and this repository becomes public, so an `_auth`,
# `_authToken` or `_password` line here would be committed and then permanent in history.
# scripts/check-tracked-files.mjs reads this one file and fails the build if one appears.
min-release-age=3
